0. Simple web stack
====================

Infrastructure diagram (whiteboard description):

[User] --HTTP request (www.foobar.com)--> [DNS server]
   |
   | DNS resolves www.foobar.com to server IP 8.8.8.8 (A record)
   v
[User] --HTTP request to 8.8.8.8--> [1 Server]
                                       |
                                       |-- Web server (Nginx)
                                       |-- Application server
                                       |-- Application files (codebase)
                                       |-- Database (MySQL)
                                       v
                                   [Response back to User]

Explanation, starting from the user:

A user opens their browser and types www.foobar.com. The computer sends a
DNS request to translate the domain name www.foobar.com into an IP
address (8.8.8.8), using an A record (www is the subdomain that points
directly to the server IP). Once the IP is known, the browser sends an
HTTP request to that IP address, on port 80 (or 443 for HTTPS).

The request arrives at the server, which is listening for connections
on port 80/443. The web server (Nginx) receives the HTTP request. Nginx
checks whether the requested resource is static (HTML, CSS, images) or
dynamic (needs code to be executed). If static, Nginx returns the file
directly. If dynamic, Nginx forwards the request to the application
server.

The application server runs the actual code (the codebase) of the
website. It processes the business logic, and if it needs data (for
example, user information, articles, products), it queries the
database (MySQL) to retrieve or store that data. The database returns
the requested data to the application server.

The application server then builds the response (usually HTML, JSON,
etc.) and sends it back to the web server, which forwards it back to
the user's browser over HTTP. The browser then renders the page for
the user.

Definitions:

- What is a server?
  A server is a physical or virtual machine (a computer) that provides
  a service (data, resources, programs) to other computers, called
  clients, over a network.

- What is the role of the domain name?
  The domain name (www.foobar.com) provides a human-readable name that
  is easier to remember than an IP address. It is used by DNS to find
  the IP address of the server that hosts the website.

- What type of DNS record is www in www.foobar.com?
  It is an A record. An A record maps a domain/subdomain name to an
  IPv4 address. Here, www is a subdomain of foobar.com that points to
  the server's IP (8.8.8.8).

- What is the role of the web server?
  The web server (Nginx) is the entry point that receives HTTP/HTTPS
  requests from users. It serves static content directly and forwards
  dynamic requests to the application server. It can also handle
  tasks like SSL termination, load balancing, and caching.

- What is the role of the application server?
  The application server executes the code (business logic) of the
  website/application. It processes the request, interacts with the
  database if needed, and generates the dynamic content (HTML, JSON,
  etc.) that is sent back through the web server to the user.

- What is the role of the database?
  The database (MySQL) stores, organizes, and manages the data used by
  the application (for example, user accounts, articles, products). It
  allows the application server to read and write persistent data.

- What is the server using to communicate with the user's computer?
  The server communicates with the user's computer using the HTTP
  protocol (or HTTPS, which is HTTP secured with SSL/TLS encryption),
  over TCP/IP.

Issues with this infrastructure:

- SPOF (Single Point of Failure):
  Since there is only one server, if that server fails (hardware
  failure, crash, network issue), the entire website becomes
  unavailable. There is no redundancy.

- Downtime when maintenance is needed:
  Since the web server, application server, and database all run on
  the same machine, any maintenance (like deploying new code and
  restarting the web server) will cause the whole website to go down,
  because everything is coupled together on one server.

- Cannot scale if too much incoming traffic:
  A single server has limited resources (CPU, RAM, disk, bandwidth).
  If traffic increases significantly, this one server cannot handle
  the load, and there is no way to add more servers to share the
  traffic (no horizontal scaling), which leads to slow performance or
  downtime.
